68
00:02:55,783 --> 00:02:58,347
and you can run a command, the git worktree

69
00:02:58,507 --> 00:03:01,952
add command, to tell it where to copy this

70
00:03:01,952 --> 00:03:04,661
repository to, so into a new folder which is

71
00:03:04,933 --> 00:03:06,279
up one level, for example,

72
00:03:06,920 --> 00:03:09,869
and which branch to check out in that

73
00:03:10,205 --> 00:03:10,622
folder.

74
00:03:11,327 --> 00:03:13,474
If you wanted to just copy the folder and

75
00:03:13,506 --> 00:03:16,006
create a new branch for it, you would add

76
00:03:16,375 --> 00:03:18,267
-b. And then the branch name here.

77
00:03:19,277 --> 00:03:22,388
So that will get Git to create a new folder

78
00:03:22,709 --> 00:03:23,415
in that path,

79
00:03:24,008 --> 00:03:26,510
copy the repository contents into that

80
00:03:26,558 --> 00:03:29,396
folder, and check out an existing or a new

81
00:03:29,541 --> 00:03:32,555
branch depending on whether -b is specified.

82
00:03:32,924 --> 00:03:35,650
It will only copy the files managed by Git

83
00:03:35,859 --> 00:03:38,248
though. In such a worktree, you can then

84
00:03:38,425 --> 00:03:40,814
work like in any other Git repository. You

85
00:03:40,911 --> 00:03:44,503
can add, you can stage files, commit files,

86
00:03:44,920 --> 00:03:46,860
and since it's a branch, you can merge that

87
00:03:46,860 --> 00:03:49,333
branch with the main repository once you're

88
00:03:49,349 --> 00:03:52,177
done. And with this new worktree feature

89
00:03:52,177 --> 00:03:55,021
supported by Codex, Codex will run the

90
00:03:55,118 --> 00:03:57,354
underlying Git commands for you behind the

91
00:03:57,405 --> 00:03:57,882
